                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="1492-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_sc.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Supercardioid" title="Supercardioid" ></span>Samson CS Series</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Detachable-Capsule Dynamic Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The Samson CS (&#8220;Capsule Select&#8221;) Series Microphone is a modular dynamic microphone with two capsule heads:</p><p><b>CS1 (Vocals)</b><br />The CS1 Capsule is designed for vocal use. </p><p>Its frequency plot shows a slight low boost in the 150Hz area, a broad presence peak centered at 6kHz with a narrow peak at 8kHz. The high end drops quickly above 10kHz.</p><p><b>CS2 (Instruments)</b><br />The CS2 Capsule is designed for instrument miking applications.</p><p>Its frequency response is similar to the CS1,  but somewhat flatter. Its low rolloff below 100Hz is less steep, and upper-mid to high response is less exaggerated.</p><p>Both capsules are supercardioid neodymium elements with steel cages and internal shockmounting.</p><p>Like Samson&#8217;s other dynamic mics, the CS Mic features a rugged, zinc-alloy die-cast enclosure and gold-plated XLR connector.</p><blockquote><p><cite>Mixdown Magazine - 2012</cite></p><p>The design is very simple and sleek. The capsules and protective cages unscrew effortlessly allowing you to change them around in a matter of moments and increasing the versatility of one microphone dramatcally.</p></blockquote><p>The mic ships with both capsules, body, and stand mount in padded zippered case.<p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Samson/CS">Samson CS Series</a> is also known as: CS-MIC.</p><p>The mic was released in 2012.</p>
